1
00:00:00,070 --> 00:00:02,290
Your cat knows its own name.

2
00:00:03,110 --> 00:00:03,980
It just doesn't come.

3
00:00:04,360 --> 00:00:06,560
And your dog grew a brand new face

4
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,190
muscle, just to work on you.

5
00:00:08,680 --> 00:00:09,500
Cats versus dogs.

6
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:12,960
Seven rounds, seven real experiments, and

7
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:14,000
one scoreboard.

8
00:00:14,330 --> 00:00:15,830
No opinions. Only science.

9
00:00:16,390 --> 00:00:17,340
Let's settle it.

10
00:00:18,190 --> 00:00:20,620
Round one. Who moved in with us first?

11
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:22,080
Dogs win this one easily.

12
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:24,720
Ancient dog DNA shows they had already

13
00:00:24,720 --> 00:00:26,860
split into five different families eleven

14
00:00:26,860 --> 00:00:29,260
thousand years ago, when every human was

15
00:00:29,260 --> 00:00:29,960
still a hunter.

16
00:00:30,270 --> 00:00:32,130
They came from wolves, and they joined us

17
00:00:32,130 --> 00:00:33,630
long before the first farm.

18
00:00:34,100 --> 00:00:34,600
Cats came later.

19
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:36,930
And they invited themselves.

20
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:38,600
When people in the Middle East started

21
00:00:38,600 --> 00:00:40,480
farming, about ten thousand years ago,

22
00:00:40,840 --> 00:00:41,460
they stored grain.

23
00:00:42,180 --> 00:00:42,820
Grain brought mice.

24
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:45,250
And mice brought wild cats.

25
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:47,180
Scientists think the cats that put up with

26
00:00:47,180 --> 00:00:49,180
people got the best hunting spot in town,

27
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:51,360
and slowly became the cat on your sofa.

28
00:00:51,660 --> 00:00:53,820
So nobody really tamed the cat.

29
00:00:54,400 --> 00:00:55,920
It moved in for the mice, and stayed.

30
00:00:56,760 --> 00:00:58,280
Point to the dog, for getting here first.

31
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:01,870
Round two. The big one.

32
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:04,070
Who is smarter?

33
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:07,320
In 2017, a team led by neuroscientist

34
00:01:07,320 --> 00:01:09,260
Suzana Herculano-Houzel, at Vanderbilt

35
00:01:09,260 --> 00:01:11,340
University, counted the neurons in the

36
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:13,080
brains of meat-eating animals.

37
00:01:13,380 --> 00:01:15,100
They looked at the cortex, the wrinkly

38
00:01:15,100 --> 00:01:16,980
outer layer that does the planning and the

39
00:01:16,980 --> 00:01:18,350
problem-solving.

40
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:20,350
A cat has about two hundred and fifty

41
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:21,370
million neurons there.

42
00:01:22,070 --> 00:01:24,330
A dog has about five hundred and thirty

43
00:01:24,330 --> 00:01:26,420
million. More than twice as many.

44
00:01:26,720 --> 00:01:27,600
And here's the twist.

45
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:30,560
A brown bear's cortex is about ten times

46
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:32,000
bigger than a cat's...

47
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:34,170
with roughly the same number of neurons.

48
00:01:34,610 --> 00:01:36,610
So a bigger brain doesn't always mean more

49
00:01:36,610 --> 00:01:37,460
thinking cells.

50
00:01:37,930 --> 00:01:39,410
Neurons aren't everything, and cats are

51
00:01:39,410 --> 00:01:40,290
clever in their own way.

52
00:01:40,650 --> 00:01:43,250
But on brain cells, round two goes to

53
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:43,710
the dog.

54
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:48,010
Round three. Who understands you?

55
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:50,530
A border collie called Chaser learned the

56
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:53,100
names of one thousand and twenty-two toys.

57
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:55,080
Her owner, psychologist John Pilley,

58
00:01:55,420 --> 00:01:56,720
trained her for three years.

59
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:58,980
Ask for any toy by name, and she

60
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:00,310
brought it back from the pile.

61
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:02,310
Then he added a toy she had never

62
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:03,810
seen, and asked for a name she had

63
00:02:03,810 --> 00:02:06,430
never heard. She picked the new one.

64
00:02:06,730 --> 00:02:07,670
She worked it out.

65
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:09,350
So cats lose this one?

66
00:02:09,750 --> 00:02:10,630
Not so fast.

67
00:02:10,930 --> 00:02:13,730
In 2019, scientists in Tokyo played cats a

68
00:02:13,730 --> 00:02:15,990
list of ordinary words, one after another,

69
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:18,040
and then the cat's own name.

70
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:19,820
The cats got bored of the words and

71
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:21,420
stopped reacting. But when they heard

72
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:23,400
their name, their ears and heads turned.

73
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:25,510
Even when a stranger said it.

74
00:02:25,820 --> 00:02:28,320
What they mostly didn't do was walk over,

75
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:29,640
or meow back.

76
00:02:29,940 --> 00:02:31,460
So your cat heard you.

77
00:02:31,780 --> 00:02:32,780
It just didn't come.

78
00:02:33,340 --> 00:02:34,980
A thousand toy names win the round for

79
00:02:34,980 --> 00:02:36,920
the dog. But the cat was listening.

80
00:02:38,390 --> 00:02:40,570
Round four. Senses.

81
00:02:41,210 --> 00:02:43,230
And the dog walks in with its famous

82
00:02:43,230 --> 00:02:43,950
nose.

83
00:02:44,250 --> 00:02:46,430
A dog's nose has up to three hundred

84
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:47,990
million smell receptors.

85
00:02:48,590 --> 00:02:49,940
Yours has about six million.

86
00:02:50,240 --> 00:02:51,740
That's why dogs can be trained to sniff

87
00:02:51,740 --> 00:02:53,360
out things we can't smell at all.

88
00:02:53,660 --> 00:02:55,880
But the cat's secret weapon is on top

89
00:02:55,880 --> 00:02:56,580
of its head.

90
00:02:56,830 --> 00:02:59,050
In the 1980s, hearing scientists Henry and

91
00:02:59,050 --> 00:03:01,050
Rickye Heffner tested how high a sound

92
00:03:01,050 --> 00:03:02,240
different animals can hear.

93
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:04,420
Dogs heard up to about forty-five thousand

94
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:05,360
vibrations a second.

95
00:03:05,500 --> 00:03:06,920
That's already twice what you can hear.

96
00:03:07,190 --> 00:03:10,290
Cats kept going, to about eighty-five

97
00:03:10,290 --> 00:03:12,430
thousand. One of the widest hearing ranges

98
00:03:12,430 --> 00:03:13,380
of any mammal.

99
00:03:13,690 --> 00:03:15,450
High enough to catch the tiny squeaks that

100
00:03:15,450 --> 00:03:17,620
mice make, sounds you will never hear.

101
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:19,640
And each ear can turn on its own,

102
00:03:19,900 --> 00:03:21,620
to find exactly where a sound came from.

103
00:03:22,320 --> 00:03:23,980
Nose to the dog, ears to the cat.

104
00:03:24,400 --> 00:03:25,200
One point each.

105
00:03:26,670 --> 00:03:28,850
Round five sounds silly.

106
00:03:29,170 --> 00:03:30,200
Who drinks better?

107
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:32,400
Watch a dog drink, and there's water

108
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:35,220
everywhere. Watch a cat, and the bowl

109
00:03:35,220 --> 00:03:36,260
barely moves.

110
00:03:36,560 --> 00:03:39,500
In 2010, engineers at MIT filmed cats

111
00:03:39,500 --> 00:03:41,380
drinking in slow motion, and found

112
00:03:41,380 --> 00:03:43,090
something nobody expected.

113
00:03:43,390 --> 00:03:45,930
The cat's tongue doesn't scoop at all.

114
00:03:46,450 --> 00:03:48,670
The tip just touches the water and pulls

115
00:03:48,670 --> 00:03:50,970
back, so fast that it drags up a

116
00:03:50,970 --> 00:03:52,100
little column of water.

117
00:03:52,400 --> 00:03:54,900
Then the cat closes its mouth at exactly

118
00:03:54,900 --> 00:03:57,480
the right moment, before gravity pulls the

119
00:03:57,480 --> 00:03:58,510
column back down.

120
00:03:58,820 --> 00:04:00,750
About four times every second.

121
00:04:01,050 --> 00:04:03,830
In 2015, a team at Virginia Tech filmed

122
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:05,010
nineteen dogs drinking.

123
00:04:05,690 --> 00:04:07,970
And here's the twist: dogs use the same

124
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:08,670
trick.

125
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:11,010
The dog curls its tongue backwards, slams

126
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:13,050
it into the water, pulls up a bigger

127
00:04:13,050 --> 00:04:14,680
column, and bites it.

128
00:04:14,990 --> 00:04:16,530
The splash is just the side effect of

129
00:04:16,530 --> 00:04:18,070
a much harder, faster tongue.

130
00:04:18,790 --> 00:04:20,330
Same physics, different style.

131
00:04:20,950 --> 00:04:22,490
Point to the cat, for neatness.

132
00:04:23,960 --> 00:04:26,530
Round six. Who controls you?

133
00:04:27,090 --> 00:04:28,470
Look into a dog's eyes when it wants

134
00:04:28,470 --> 00:04:31,500
something. Those big, sad puppy eyes.

135
00:04:31,860 --> 00:04:34,260
In 2019, scientists compared the faces of

136
00:04:34,260 --> 00:04:36,860
dogs and wolves, and found a tiny muscle

137
00:04:36,860 --> 00:04:39,000
above the eye that dogs have, and wolves

138
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:39,760
mostly don't.

139
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:41,240
It lifts the inner eyebrow.

140
00:04:42,060 --> 00:04:44,700
It makes the eyes look bigger, sadder, and

141
00:04:44,700 --> 00:04:45,810
more like a baby's.

142
00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:48,030
And in animal shelters, dogs that made

143
00:04:48,030 --> 00:04:49,570
that face more often found new homes

144
00:04:49,570 --> 00:04:50,270
faster.

145
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:51,740
Scientists think that's how the muscle

146
00:04:51,740 --> 00:04:53,682
spread: thousands of years of people

147
00:04:53,682 --> 00:04:55,990
choosing the dogs with the saddest eyes.

148
00:04:56,290 --> 00:04:58,190
But the cat has its own weapon.

149
00:04:58,910 --> 00:04:59,820
The purr.

150
00:05:00,170 --> 00:05:02,550
In 2009, researchers at the University of

151
00:05:02,550 --> 00:05:05,170
Sussex recorded cats purring for food, and

152
00:05:05,170 --> 00:05:06,540
played the sounds to fifty people.

153
00:05:06,910 --> 00:05:09,230
Hidden inside the purr was a high little

154
00:05:09,230 --> 00:05:11,270
cry, at almost the same pitch as a

155
00:05:11,270 --> 00:05:12,400
human baby's.

156
00:05:12,740 --> 00:05:14,400
People rated those purrs as more urgent,

157
00:05:14,740 --> 00:05:16,170
even people who had never owned a cat.

158
00:05:16,520 --> 00:05:18,060
Dogs have a face built for you.

159
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:20,140
Cats have a sound built for you.

160
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:21,440
That's a draw.

161
00:05:21,620 --> 00:05:22,280
One point each.

162
00:05:23,750 --> 00:05:27,260
Final round. Who actually loves you?

163
00:05:27,850 --> 00:05:28,750
Dogs, everyone agrees.

164
00:05:29,390 --> 00:05:31,130
In a strange room, a dog explores while

165
00:05:31,130 --> 00:05:33,050
its owner is there, gets upset when the

166
00:05:33,050 --> 00:05:34,830
owner leaves, and runs to them when they

167
00:05:34,830 --> 00:05:35,530
come back.

168
00:05:35,790 --> 00:05:37,430
Scientists call that a secure bond.

169
00:05:37,850 --> 00:05:38,990
It's the same test they use with human

170
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:39,690
babies.

171
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:42,020
Cats were supposed to fail it.

172
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,420
In 2019, at Oregon State University,

173
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:46,740
Kristyn Vitale put kittens in the same

174
00:05:46,740 --> 00:05:49,200
test. Two minutes with their owner, two

175
00:05:49,200 --> 00:05:51,370
minutes alone, then the owner comes back.

176
00:05:51,670 --> 00:05:53,450
About two out of three kittens greeted

177
00:05:53,450 --> 00:05:55,470
their owner, then calmly went back to

178
00:05:55,470 --> 00:05:57,280
exploring. A secure bond.

179
00:05:57,580 --> 00:05:59,680
That's almost exactly the share found in

180
00:05:59,680 --> 00:06:00,470
human babies.

181
00:06:00,780 --> 00:06:02,340
The cat doesn't love you less.

182
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:04,260
It just shows it more quietly.

183
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:06,480
Point to the cat, for the biggest

184
00:06:06,480 --> 00:06:07,180
surprise.

185
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:08,840
Final score. Dog, five.

186
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:09,900
Cat, four.

187
00:06:09,960 --> 00:06:12,220
The dog takes history, brains and words.

188
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:15,060
The cat takes the ears, the tidy drinking,

189
00:06:15,460 --> 00:06:16,720
and the quiet kind of love.

190
00:06:17,030 --> 00:06:18,090
Which side are you on?

191
00:06:18,510 --> 00:06:19,330
Tell us in the comments.

192
00:06:19,730 --> 00:06:21,270
And now, go call your cat.

193
00:06:21,850 --> 00:06:22,550
It heard you.

194
00:06:22,910 --> 00:06:24,850
It's just... deciding.
